The `insert` function takes an item as input and inserts it at the end of the linked list. It first checks if the linked list has reached its maximum capacity of 100,000 elements. If so, it displays a message and returns. Otherwise, it creates a new node with the given item, sets its `next` pointer to `nullptr`, and then inserts the node at the end of the linked list. If the linked list is empty, the new node becomes both the head and the tail of the list. Otherwise, the `next` pointer of the current tail node is set to the new node, and the tail is updated to point to the new node. Finally, the size of the linked list is incremented.

The `display` function is used to print the elements of the linked list.

