Write a C++ function to find the maximum element in an array of integers. The function should handle negative numbers and return the maximum element as an integer.

You should implement the function using a divide-and-conquer approach. Divide the array into two halves and recursively find the maximum element in each half. Then, compare the maximum elements of the two halves and return the larger one as the maximum element of the whole array.

Your function should have the following signature:
```cpp
int findMax(int arr[], int start, int end);
```

Additionally, you should handle the case when the array is empty (i.e., `start > end`). In this case, your function should return a special value, such as `INT_MIN`, to indicate that there is no maximum element.

The input array will have a maximum length of 10^6 elements.

Here is an example of how the function should be used:
```cpp
int arr[] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
int size = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);
int max = findMax(arr, 0, size - 1);
std::cout << "Maximum element: " << max << std::endl;
```

Please provide the implementation of the `findMax` function.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <climits>

int findMax(int arr[], int start, int end) {
    if (start > end) {
        // Array is empty, return special value
        return INT_MIN;
    }

    if (start == end) {
        // Only one element in the array, return it
        return arr[start];
    }

    int mid = (start + end) / 2;
    int maxLeft = findMax(arr, start, mid);
    int maxRight = findMax(arr, mid + 1, end);

    return std::max(maxLeft, maxRight);
}

int main() {
    int arr[] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
    int size = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);
    int max = findMax(arr, 0, size - 1);
    std::cout << "Maximum element: " << max << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

